    <!-- GLP-1 -->
    <div class="glp-section reveal">
        <p class="section-stamp">GLP-1 Friendly / The Science, Plainly</p>

        <div class="glp-hero">
            <p class="glp-tag">Satiety Science / GLP-1</p>
            <h2 class="glp-headline">Your body makes its own<br><em>satiety signal.</em> Feed it.</h2>
            <p class="glp-sub">GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) is a hormone your gut produces naturally when you eat — especially when you eat certain fibers. It signals fullness to your brain, slows digestion, and supports healthy blood glucose response. Pharmaceutical GLP-1 drugs mimic this signal artificially. The research below is about triggering it the way your body was designed to — through food.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Plain English -->
        <div class="glp-plain">
            <h3 class="glp-plain-title">What is GLP-1, in plain terms?</h3>
            <p>When you eat, your gut sends signals to your brain that say "we're getting food — start feeling full." GLP-1 is one of those signals. It's released by special cells in your intestinal lining called <strong>L-cells</strong>, and it does three things: tells your brain you're full, slows down how fast food leaves your stomach, and helps your body manage blood sugar more gradually.</p>
            <p>Most people have heard of GLP-1 through the drugs Ozempic and Wegovy — those are synthetic GLP-1 receptor agonists that activate the same pathway at much higher intensity than food normally would. What's less discussed is that your body produces GLP-1 naturally, and the strength of that natural signal is significantly influenced by what you eat — specifically, by fermentable dietary fiber.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- 3-step mechanism -->
        <div class="glp-steps">
            <div class="glp-step">
                <div class="glp-step-num">01</div>
                <p class="glp-step-title">Fiber reaches the colon intact</p>
                <p class="glp-step-text">Fermentable fibers — including <strong>beta-glucan, resistant starch, and soluble fiber</strong> — are not digested in the small intestine. They pass through to the colon where gut bacteria are waiting to use them as fuel.<sup class="cite"><a href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/endocrinology/articles/10.3389/fendo.2026.1880500/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[1]</a></sup></p>
            </div>
            <div class="glp-step">
                <div class="glp-step-num">02</div>
                <p class="glp-step-title">Bacteria ferment fiber into SCFAs</p>
                <p class="glp-step-text">Gut bacteria break down fiber into <strong>short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs)</strong> — mainly butyrate, propionate, and acetate. Butyrate feeds colon cells directly. Propionate and acetate enter the bloodstream and travel to the liver and brain.<sup class="cite"><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11278623/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[2]</a></sup></p>
            </div>
            <div class="glp-step">
                <div class="glp-step-num">03</div>
                <p class="glp-step-title">SCFAs trigger GLP-1 release</p>
                <p class="glp-step-text">SCFAs bind to <strong>free fatty acid receptors (FFAR2/3)</strong> on L-cells in the gut lining. This triggers GLP-1 secretion — your natural fullness signal — along with other satiety hormones including PYY.<sup class="cite"><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11278623/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[2]</a></sup></p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- What the research actually shows -->
        <div class="glp-explainer">
            <div class="glp-card">
                <p class="glp-card-label">Beta-Glucan (Oat) + GLP-1</p>
                <h3 class="glp-card-title">Direct clinical evidence in humans</h3>
                <p class="glp-card-text">A 2024 randomized crossover trial in healthy adults found that beta-glucan-enriched oat bread significantly affected gastric emptying rate and GLP-1 response compared to a control bread.<sup class="cite"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10949669/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[3]</a></sup> A separate trial in subjects with type 2 diabetes showed that 5g of oat beta-glucan daily for 12 weeks produced significantly higher GLP-1 and PYY levels in the treatment group vs. control (p&lt;0.01).<sup class="cite"><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1756464620305351" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[4]</a></sup> GUTE Crunch contains <strong>both oat flour and oat bran</strong> — the two richest sources of beta-glucan in the formula.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="glp-card">
                <p class="glp-card-label">Resistant Starch RS2 + Microbiome</p>
                <h3 class="glp-card-title">Feeds bacteria linked to higher GLP-1</h3>
                <p class="glp-card-text">A 2022 randomized clinical trial found that a resistant starch blend (including banana-source RS) significantly increased butyrate production and shifted gut bacteria populations — with higher proportions of Bifidobacterium and Akkermansia positively correlated with GLP-1 levels.<sup class="cite"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9559413/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[5]</a></sup> GUTE Crunch uses <strong>green banana flour</strong> as its RS2 source — unripe banana specifically, before the starch converts to sugar.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Fiber diversity note -->
        <div class="glp-plain" style="margin-top:0;">
            <h3 class="glp-plain-title">Why fiber diversity matters</h3>
            <p>A 2026 scoping review in <em>Frontiers in Endocrinology</em> analyzing 49 publications found that <strong>not all fibers affect GLP-1 equally</strong> — fiber type, molecular weight, fermentation rate, and individual microbiome composition all influence the response.<sup class="cite"><a href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/endocrinology/articles/10.3389/fendo.2026.1880500/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[1]</a></sup> The review identified soluble dextrins and beta-glucan among the fibers with the most consistent evidence for GLP-1 and satiety effects.</p>
            <p>This is why GUTE Crunch uses <strong>five distinct fiber types</strong> rather than a high dose of a single source. Different bacteria specialize in different substrates. A diverse fiber stack means more of your microbiome is active — and more consistent SCFA production across the range of bacterial populations present in any given person's gut. A single fiber at a high dose feeds fewer populations, and individual response varies significantly depending on which bacteria you have.<sup class="cite"><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9559413/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">[5]</a></sup></p>
        </div>

        <!-- References -->
        <div class="glp-refs">
            <p class="glp-refs-label">References</p>
            <div class="glp-ref">
                <span class="glp-ref-num">[1]</span>
                <p class="glp-ref-text">Frontiers in Endocrinology (2026). <em>Dietary fibers to boost endogenous GLP-1 secretion and satiety: a scoping-review.</em> <a href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/endocrinology/articles/10.3389/fendo.2026.1880500/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">frontiersin.org →</a></p>
            </div>
            <div class="glp-ref">
                <span class="glp-ref-num">[2]</span>
