            </div>
            <div class="glp-ref">
                <span class="glp-ref-num">[3]</span>
                <p class="glp-ref-text">Goux et al., PMC (2024). <em>The acute effect of a β-glucan-enriched oat bread on gastric emptying, GLP-1 response, and postprandial glycaemia: a randomised crossover trial in healthy adults.</em> <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10949669/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov →</a></p>
            </div>
            <div class="glp-ref">
                <span class="glp-ref-num">[4]</span>
                <p class="glp-ref-text">Zhao et al., <em>Journal of Functional Foods</em> (2020). <em>Effect of dietary supplementation with oat β-glucan for 3 months in subjects with type 2 diabetes: a randomized, double-blind, controlled clinical trial.</em> <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1756464620305351" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sciencedirect.com →</a></p>
            </div>
            <div class="glp-ref">
                <span class="glp-ref-num">[5]</span>
                <p class="glp-ref-text">Baxter et al., <em>Frontiers in Nutrition</em> (2022). <em>The gastrointestinal and microbiome impact of a resistant starch blend from potato, banana, and apple fibers: a randomized clinical trial.</em> <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9559413/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov →</a></p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p class="glp-disclaimer">These statements have not been evaluated by the Food and Drug Administration. GUTE Crunch is not intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease. The citations above reflect peer-reviewed nutritional research; individual response to dietary fiber varies based on gut microbiome composition, overall diet, and other factors.</p>
    </div>

